// The driver child, from the launcher's side (spec §2.5, §9, §12). This file
// is the half that knows no hardware: it hands a device window to a child, and
// the child drives the device. A window that arrives as a capability is a
// window somebody can hand over.
//
// Expected transcript:
//
//   SOS driverchild: created            <- an inert process, table empty
//   SOS driverchild: gave the window    <- `Transfer` spent; root lost it
//   SOS driverchild: gave the line      <- root bound it; the child holds it
//   SOS driverchild: started
//   SOS echo: window mapped             <- the child, mapping what it was given
//   SOS echo: driver up
//   Zq7#                                <- the harness's own bytes, echoed by a
//                                          process that is not root, through a
//                                          device root gave away
//   SOS echo: done 4 bytes on line <N>  <- the board's console line
//   SOS: process exit: code=...
//   SOS driverchild: root observed child status=65536
//
// What each step rules out. `gave the window` is `ProcessOp.Give` over an
// `IoMemory`, which spends the universal `Transfer` bit on the window and moves
// it: root's wrapper is disarmed before the syscall and root can map that
// window nowhere afterwards. `window mapped` is the child calling
// `Process.map(iomemory:)` on its own Process handle: what crosses the boundary
// is authority over a device, and where the row goes is then the holder's
// business. `gave the line` is the same move for the line:
// `System.interrupt_bind` in root, then `ProcessOp.Give` over the Interrupt,
// which spends `Transfer` and moves its one name. The echoed bytes exercise
// every step at once (give, drain, map, enable, park, wake and ack) across a
// process boundary, on a device the kernel has no driver for.
//
// The park is the child's death and there is no timer. A driver waits on the
// harness typing at a real serial port, which takes far longer than any fixed
// launcher deadline, so a timer would have to be guessed at. And §9a's console
// handover has one bounded exception, a kernel whose armed tick narrates over a
// process that owns the device; a case whose transcript matters after handover
// arms none.
//
// What root does not do is the other half of the claim. It never maps the
// device, never reads a register and never learns the UART's address:
// `UART_BASE` appears nowhere in this file. Root's whole part is deciding who
// gets the window and the line. The line is the one thing it does name: root
// holds `SystemRight.InterruptBind`, binds it with `System.interrupt_bind` and
// gives the bound Interrupt away, so the driver holds that line and no
// authority to bind another (spec §9). A line number is a fact about the board,
// so it comes from the one C file this package links per target, and this file
// stays arch-free.

import sos.{System, Process, Memory, IoMemory, BootHandle, Waiter, WaitResult,
            WaitPayload, SosStatus, SystemRight}

static EXIT_OK: UInt = 0
static EXIT_FAILED: UInt = 1

/// The key this launcher attaches the child's Process handle under: a number
/// that appears nowhere else in the transcript, so reading it back proves the
/// record came from this attachment.
static KEY: UInt = 66

/// Root's config, and the whole of the contract with the driver. `TAG_BOOT` is
/// the tag the child's System handle travels under and is what `start` names;
/// `TAG_WINDOW` is the device window, which the child meets at its drain.
///
/// The two tags are ordered by what consumes them, not by when they are given:
/// resolving a boot tag consumes its record (the register is the delivery), so
/// the System handle never appears at the drain and the window is the only
/// record the child ever sees. That is why the driver's drain loop is one call
/// and needs no tag test, as in the root-as-driver twin.
static TAG_BOOT: UInt = 31
static TAG_WINDOW: UInt = 32
static TAG_LINE: UInt = 33

// The console UART's interrupt line on the board this image runs on, answered
// by the target's own C file (`tests/irq-lines`).
extern "C" {
    func driver_irq_line() -> UInt
}

/// The policy is a whitelist. What a driver child of this launcher may ask its
/// System object for: print, learn its own Process object, read the clock.
/// `Shutdown`, `ProcessCreate`, `InterruptBind` and `Mint` are absent by
/// silence, which is the keep mask's polarity: a System right added later is
/// denied to every child configured with this mask. The child is given its line
/// and cannot bind another.
///
/// `ClockGet` is in the mask and this driver never spends it, deliberately: the
/// mask is what a launcher grants a driver, and a driver with a device to
/// service plausibly wants a timeout. Narrowing it to what one program happens
/// to call would make the policy a transcript of the program.
///
/// `Transfer` is in the mask because it is the bit `give` checks on the thing
/// being given.
static CHILD_SYSTEM: UInt32 = (SystemRight.Debug | SystemRight.ProcessSelf
                               | SystemRight.ClockGet | SystemRight.Transfer)

/// The §8 status word out of the record the kernel copied into this process's
/// memory while the thread was parked.
///
/// The other three arms panic: this program attached exactly one thing, so a
/// record of any other kind would mean the kernel reported an attachment nobody
/// made, a bug in the pair rather than a condition to handle.
func status_of(r: &WaitResult) -> UInt {
    match r.what {
        case Process(status) -> status,
        case _ -> panic("this launcher attached only a Process"),
    }
}

@export("_start")
func _start(boot_handle: UInt) {
    let system = System(boot_handle: boot_handle)

    let proc = try system.process_self() catch {
        let _ = system.debug_print("SOS driverchild: no process handle\n")
        let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }

    // The boot set, in the build's row order: the child's image blob, the RAM
    // it will run in, and the console's register page. The third row is a
    // device row, so the kernel minted an `IoMemory` for it rather than a
    // `Memory`. Root's config says which ordinal is which.
    var image = try proc.boot_handle_next() catch {
        print("SOS driverchild: no image region: {}\n", error)
        let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }
    var dest = try proc.boot_handle_next() catch {
        print("SOS driverchild: no destination region: {}\n", error)
        let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }
    var device = try proc.boot_handle_next() catch {
        print("SOS driverchild: no device window: {}\n", error)
        let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }
    guard let image_mem = image.take_memory() else {
        let _ = system.debug_print("SOS driverchild: image record is not a region\n")
        let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }
    guard let dest_mem = dest.take_memory() else {
        let _ = system.debug_print("SOS driverchild: dest record is not a region\n")
        let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }
    guard let window = device.take_iomemory() else {
        let _ = system.debug_print("SOS driverchild: device record is not a window\n")
        let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }

    let child = try system.process_create(image: &image_mem,
                                          memory: &dest_mem) catch {
        print("SOS driverchild: create failed: {}\n", error)
        let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }
    let _ = system.debug_print("SOS driverchild: created\n")

    // The supervision attachment, before the start: root will be woken by the
    // child's ending, whatever ends it. A driver that faults on its first
    // register read reports here exactly as one that finishes does, so this
    // park needs no deadline to be safe.
    let waiter = try proc.waiter_create() catch {
        print("SOS driverchild: no waiter: {}\n", error)
        let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }
    try waiter.add(process: &child, key: KEY) catch {
        print("SOS driverchild: attach failed: {}\n", error)
        let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }

    // The furnishing. The masked System is the usual flow. The window is the
    // new part: `give(iomemory:)` spends `IoMemoryRight.Transfer` on the thing
    // being given and `ProcessRight.Give` on the child, moves the word into the
    // child's table under `TAG_WINDOW`, and consumes root's wrapper. The
    // transfer funnel disarms it before the syscall, so nothing in this file
    // can touch the window below this point. What root gives away it cannot
    // keep.
    //
    // Root does not map it anywhere first. It could (a `map` before a `give`
    // is two ops with no rule against them), but that would leave a device row
    // in root's own domain naming registers root has no business reaching. The
    // window must end up in exactly one address space.
    let for_child = try system.mint(rights: CHILD_SYSTEM) catch {
        print("SOS driverchild: mint failed: {}\n", error)
        let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }
    try child.give(system: move for_child, tag: TAG_BOOT) catch {
        print("SOS driverchild: give system failed: {}\n", error.0)
        let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }
    try child.give(iomemory: move window, tag: TAG_WINDOW) catch {
        print("SOS driverchild: give window failed: {}\n", error.0)
        let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }
    let _ = system.debug_print("SOS driverchild: gave the window\n")

    // The line. Root binds it and gives the one Interrupt handle away under its
    // own tag, behind the window in the child's boot set. The give moves the
    // Interrupt's one name: the line stays bound, now to the child, and root
    // can neither ack it nor bind the line again while the child lives. When
    // the child's handle goes (its release, or its teardown) the line is
    // unbound and free.
    let line = try system.interrupt_bind(line: driver_irq_line()) catch {
        print("SOS driverchild: bind failed: {}\n", error)
        let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }
    try child.give(interrupt: move line, tag: TAG_LINE) catch {
        print("SOS driverchild: give line failed: {}\n", error.0)
        let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }
    let _ = system.debug_print("SOS driverchild: gave the line\n")

    // The child's own authority over itself is what makes it a driver, and root
    // does not grant it here. One Process right is spent inside the child:
    // `Map`, to install the window in its own domain. It arrives on the handle
    // `SystemOp.ProcessSelf` mints for it, which carries the Process default
    // set. So the mask above does not reach that bit: only the child can narrow
    // the handle it derives, through `ProcessSelf`'s own keep mask (spec §9).
    // This driver child is exactly the process that should hold it.
    try child.start(boot_tag: TAG_BOOT) catch {
        print("SOS driverchild: start failed: {}\n", error)
        let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }
    let _ = system.debug_print("SOS driverchild: started\n")

    // The park. Nothing here is armed and nothing here is bound: the only
    // interrupt in the machine is the child's, and the only thing that can end
    // this call is the child ending. Everything between this line and the next
    // belongs to the driver, and to the device it owns.
    let record = try waiter.wait() catch {
        print("SOS driverchild: wait failed: {}\n", error)
        let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }
    if record.key != KEY {
        print("SOS driverchild: woke on key {}, not {}\n", record.key, KEY)
        let _ = system.shutdown(EXIT_FAILED)
        return
    }

    print("SOS driverchild: root observed child status={}\n", status_of(&record))
    print("SOS driverchild: done\n")
    let _ = proc.exit(EXIT_OK)
}
